  <language>en_US</language>
  <keyword>gammaincinv</keyword>
  <short_description>Inverse of the regularized incomplete gamma function.</short_description>
  <syntax>
    <syntax_item>X = gammaincinv(Y, A)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>X = gammaincinv(Y, A, type)</syntax_item>
  </syntax>
  <param_input>
    <param_input_item>
      <param_name>Y</param_name>
      <param_description>probabilities in [0, 1]: a real single or double array.</param_description>
    </param_input_item>
    <param_input_item>
      <param_name>A</param_name>
      <param_description>a real nonnegative array of the size of <b>Y</b>, or a scalar.</param_description>
    </param_input_item>
    <param_input_item>
      <param_name>type</param_name>
      <param_description><b>'lower'</b> (default) or <b>'upper'</b>.</param_description>
    </param_input_item>
  </param_input>
  <param_output>
    <param_output_item>
      <param_name>X</param_name>
      <param_description>values such that <b>gammainc(X, A, type) = Y</b>.</param_description>
    </param_output_item>
  </param_output>
  <description>
    <p><b>X = gammaincinv(Y, A)</b> returns <b>X</b> such that <b>gammainc(X, A) = Y</b>. With <b>'upper'</b>, <b>X</b> solves <b>gammainc(X, A, 'upper') = Y</b>, which keeps full accuracy for small upper tail probabilities.</p>
    <p><b>Y</b> and <b>A</b> must have the same size, or one of them is a scalar. <b>gammaincinv(0, A)</b> is <b>0</b> and <b>gammaincinv(1, A)</b> is <b>Inf</b>.</p>
    <p>Newton iterations on <b>log(X)</b> start from the best of three approximations and stay inside a bracket narrowed by bisection.</p>
  </description>
